WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.981
The checklist.

2
00:00:01.261 --> 00:00:10.861
Google’s engineering practices list what reviewers look for: design, functionality, complexity, tests, naming, comments, style, and documentation.

3
00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:12.443
Keep that as the base.

4
00:00:12.723 --> 00:00:18.696
For agent-written changes, add six questions, in this order: Does it match the spec, and only the spec?

5
00:00:18.976 --> 00:00:22.923
List the files you expected to change; compare with the files that did.

6
00:00:23.203 --> 00:00:25.229
Every extra file needs a reason.

7
00:00:25.509 --> 00:00:34.341
“For consistency” refactors, renamed variables, and reformatted files are scope creep: they enlarge the review and hide the real change.

8
00:00:34.621 --> 00:00:35.901
Did any test change?

9
00:00:36.181 --> 00:00:38.613
Read test diffs first (lesson 7).

10
00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:45.571
A changed expectation, a new skip, or a deleted test needs your judgment before anything else means anything.

11
00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:47.899
Were safeguards removed or weakened?

12
00:00:48.179 --> 00:01:03.411
Look at removed lines, not just added ones: input validation, permission and auth checks, expiry checks, rate limits, error handling replaced by a bare except: pass, a timeout raised to “fix” a flaky test, a security header dropped.

13
00:01:03.691 --> 00:01:07.104
Agents remove obstacles; some obstacles are the point.

14
00:01:07.384 --> 00:01:08.557
New dependencies?

15
00:01:08.837 --> 00:01:10.416
Any change to requirements.

16
00:01:10.696 --> 00:01:12.253
txt, pyproject.

17
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:13.643
toml, package.

18
00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:15.565
json, or a lockfile.

19
00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:24.741
Spracklen and colleagues (2024) tested 16 code-generating models and found hallucinated package names in at least 5.

20
00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:29.480
2% of suggestions on average for commercial models and 21.

21
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.776
7% for open-source models; a name that doesn’t exist can be registered by an attacker.

22
00:01:36.056 --> 00:01:40.301
Check that each package exists, is maintained, and is actually needed.

23
00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:42.309
Security-sensitive patterns?

24
00:01:42.589 --> 00:01:58.589
Shell commands built from strings, eval, disabled certificate checks, secrets or tokens in code, broadened CORS or permissions, SQL built by concatenation, new network calls, changes to CI workflows or agent configuration (.

25
00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.619
github/workflows/,.

26
00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:02.179
claude/settings.

27
00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:03.696
json, hooks).

28
00:02:03.976 --> 00:02:05.939
Would you understand this in six months?

29
00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:15.520
Agents can write code that works and is still wrong for your codebase: a second way of doing something you already do one way, an abstraction nobody asked for.

30
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.808
If you couldn’t explain it to a teammate, you can’t maintain it.
